---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const checks = [
  "I feel heaviness, pressure, or a dragging feeling in my pelvis",
  "I feel or see a bulge in or at the opening of my vagina",
  "My symptoms get worse as the day goes on, or after standing or lifting",
  "I leak urine when I cough, sneeze, laugh, or exercise",
  "I have trouble fully emptying my bladder or bowels",
  "Sex has become uncomfortable, or sensation has changed",
  "These feelings have lasted more than a few weeks",
];
---
<Base
  title="Symptoms and self check"
  description="What pelvic organ prolapse commonly feels like, plus a private two minute self check. Your answers are never stored or shared."
  current="/symptoms/"
>
  <div class="narrow">
    <span class="label">Symptoms</span>
    <h1>What prolapse actually feels like</h1>
    <p>
      Many women carry these symptoms for years before mentioning them to
      anyone. Some are embarrassed. Some assume it is just part of getting
      older. Some genuinely have no words for what they are feeling. You deserve
      better than years of wondering, so here is what prolapse commonly feels
      like.
    </p>

    <h2>Common experiences</h2>
    <ul class="spaced">
      <li>Heaviness, pressure, or a dragging feeling in the pelvis or vagina</li>
      <li>A sense of a bulge or lump in the vagina. Some women can see or feel tissue at the opening</li>
      <li>The feeling that something is falling out, especially by the end of the day or after long standing</li>
      <li>Leaking urine when you cough, sneeze, laugh, or exercise</li>
      <li>Trouble fully emptying the bladder or bowels, or needing to press on the vagina to pass a bowel movement</li>
      <li>Discomfort or reduced sensation during sex</li>
      <li>A low back ache that eases when you lie down</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Symptoms are usually mildest in the morning and build through the day.
      Having one or two of these does not mean you have prolapse, since several
      other conditions cause similar feelings. All of them are still worth
      mentioning to a healthcare provider.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Two minutes, completely private</span>
        <h2 id="selfcheck-h">Is it worth talking to someone?</h2>
        <p>
          This is not a diagnosis and it cannot tell you whether you have
          prolapse. It just helps you notice patterns in what you have been
          feeling, so you can decide whether to raise it with a doctor or a
          pelvic health physiotherapist.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="privacy-note">
        <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="5" y="10" width="14" height="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M8 10V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg>
        Your answers stay on your device. They are never saved, stored, or shared with anyone.
      </p>

      <form id="selfcheck" aria-labelledby="selfcheck-h">
        <fieldset style="border:none;padding:0;margin:1rem 0 0;">
          <legend style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;">Check all that apply to you</legend>
          {checks.map((c, i) => (
            <label class="check-item">
              <input type="checkbox" name={"c" + i} />
              <span>{c}</span>
            </label>
          ))}
        </fieldset>
        <p class="actions" style="margin-top:1.4rem;">
          <button type="submit" class="btn">See what this might mean</button>
        </p>
      </form>

      <div id="result-many" class="check-result callout" hidden aria-live="polite">
        <p>
          <strong>Several of the things you noticed are commonly associated with
          pelvic floor conditions, including prolapse.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          That does not mean you have prolapse. It does mean this is worth a
          conversation. A doctor or pelvic health physiotherapist can assess you
          properly, and there are effective options at every stage.
        </p>
        <p>
          It may also help to talk with women who have been where you
          are. <a href="/community/">Our community</a> is free and private.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="result-few" class="check-result callout" hidden aria-live="polite">
        <p>
          <strong>Nothing here points strongly toward prolapse.</strong> A quick
          check like this cannot rule anything out, though, and you know your
          body better than any list does.
        </p>
        <p>
          If something feels off, or it is bothering you, it is always worth
          raising with your doctor. No symptom is too small or too embarrassing
          to mention.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="narrow">
    <h2>See a doctor promptly if</h2>
    <ul class="spaced">
      <li>You suddenly cannot urinate or empty your bowels</li>
      <li>You have heavy vaginal bleeding</li>
      <li>Prolapsed tissue is outside the body and has become painful or ulcerated, or will not go back in</li>
      <li>You have a fever or worsening pain after a pelvic procedure or surgery</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var form = document.getElementById("selfcheck");
      if (!form) return;
      form.addEventListener("submit", function (e) {
        e.preventDefault();
        var checked = form.querySelectorAll("input:checked").length;
        var many = document.getElementById("result-many");
        var few = document.getElementById("result-few");
        var show = checked >= 3 ? many : few;
        var hide = checked >= 3 ? few : many;
        hide.hidden = true;
        show.hidden = false;
        show.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "nearest" });
      });
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
